/*!
    \title Qt OpenAPI
    \page qtopenapi-index.html
    \brief A module for generating Qt HTTP clients using OpenAPI
           and Qt Network RESTful APIs.

    The Qt OpenAPI module provides functionality for generating Qt HTTP clients
    using Qt Network RESTful APIs. The main module features are the
    Qt OpenAPI generator and pre-generated \c OpenApiCommon library.

    The Qt OpenAPI generator is a plugin to the
    \l{https://openapi-generator.tech/}{OpenAPI generator}. It allows you to
    autogenerate Qt HTTP clients in C++ using \l{Qt Network} APIs such as
    \l QRestAccessManager.

    \note Qt OpenAPI in \QtVer is in \e{Technology Preview}, excluding its
    API from \l[QtDoc]{Compatibility promises}{Qt's compatibility promises}.

    \section1 Using the module
    Installation of the following packages is necessary to use the Qt OpenAPI
    module:
    \list
        \li \l {https://openapi-generator.tech/}{OpenAPI generator}
            (requires OpenAPI 7.12.0 or above).
        \li \l {https://maven.apache.org/}{Maven plugin}
            (requires Maven 3.0 or above).
        \li \l {https://openjdk.org/projects/jdk/}{Java JDK}
            (requires JDK 17 or above).
    \endlist
    Once you have the required installations, the Qt OpenAPI generator
    is ready for use within your projects.
    To generate client code from an OpenAPI specification
    using the Qt OpenAPI Generator, call the
    \l qt_add_openapi_client function inside your project's
    CMakeLists.txt file. See \l {Building with CMake} for details.

    \section2 Building with CMake
    \section3 Using the \c qt_add_openapi_client function
    Use the \c find_package() command to locate the needed module
    components in the Qt6 package. Then call the
    \l qt_add_openapi_client function to generate a required
    Qt HTTP client library. See the full CMake code example below:
    \code
        cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.22)

        project(openapiApplication LANGUAGES CXX)

        set(CMAKE_AUTOUIC ON)
        set(CMAKE_AUTOMOC ON)
        set(CMAKE_AUTORCC ON)

        set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)
        set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)

        find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS Core OpenApiCommon OpenApiTools)

        qt_add_executable(openapiApplication
          main.cpp
        )
        qt_add_library(generatedLibrary)
        qt_add_openapi_client(generatedLibrary
            SPEC_FILE
                ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/spec.yaml
        )
        target_link_libraries(openapiApplication Qt6::Core generatedLibrary)

        include(GNUInstallDirs)
        install(TARGETS openapiApplication
            LIBRARY DESTINATION ${CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR}
            RUNTIME DESTINATION ${CMAKE_INSTALL_BINDIR}
        )
    \endcode
    \note \c OpenApiTools library doesn't need to be linked to the resulting
    \c openapiApplication target. However, it's still required for enabling
    the \l qt_add_openapi_client cmake function.
    \note \c OpenApiCommon library is linked to the target by the
    \l qt_add_openapi_client call.

    \section3 Calling the Qt OpenAPI Generator from the command line
    To generate client code from an OpenAPI specification
    using the Qt OpenAPI Generator, you should run the following
    command:
    \badcode
        java -cp <path-to-installed-openapigen>/openapi-generator-cli.jar:<path-to-installed-qt>/<qt-version>/libexec/cpp-qt6-client-openapi-generator.jar \
        org.openapitools.codegen.OpenAPIGenerator \
        generate -g cpp-qt6-client \
        --additional-properties=cppCommonNamespace=QtOpenApiCommon,prefix=QOAI \
        -o generator_output/ -i file.yaml
    \endcode
    The command above adds the Qt OpenAPI generator to the classpath,
    which makes it visible to the upstream OpenAPI generator,
    then invokes main class \c org.openapitools.codegen.OpenAPIGenerator.
    Setting the Qt OpenAPI generator's name \c cpp-qt6-client
    to the \c -g parameter makes sure it will be found and called.

    To generate client code from an extra large OpenAPI specification file,
    add the \c maxYamlCodePoints option to the command line, see below:
    \badcode
        java -DmaxYamlCodePoints=99999999 <other arguments>
    \endcode
    \note Always add
    \c {--additional-properties=cppCommonNamespace=QtOpenApiCommon,prefix=QOAI}
    if you want to link the pre-generated \c Qt6::OpenApiCommon
    library to the project, because it was built with these parameters,
    and you'll get compilation errors if you provide different values.

    The generator creates the client library in
    the \c generator_output folder. Add the \c generator_output
    folder to the project, as shown in the following example:
    \code
        find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS Core OpenApiCommon)
        add_subdirectory(generator_output/client)
        add_executable(example_binary
          main.cpp
        )
        target_link_libraries(Qt6OpenAPIClient PUBLIC Qt6::OpenApiCommon)
        target_link_libraries(example_binary PRIVATE Qt6::Core Qt6OpenAPIClient)
    \endcode

    If you want to see all generation options, you can use the
    \c config-help option, see below:
    \badcode
        java -cp <path-to-installed-openapigen>/openapi-generator-cli.jar:<path-to-installed-qt>/<qt-version>/libexec/cpp-qt6-client-openapi-generator.jar \
        org.openapitools.codegen.OpenAPIGenerator config-help -g cpp-qt6-client
    \endcode
    \note You can find more information about the generator usage in the
    \l{https://openapi-generator.tech/docs/usage}{OpenAPI generator documentation}.

    \section1 Examples

    \list
        \li \l{Qt OpenAPI Examples}
    \endlist

    \section1 Reference

    This module provides build tools (CMake commands) rather than a runtime
    C++ API. See \l{CMake Commands in Qt6 OpenApi} for the available commands.

    \section1 Licenses and Attributions

    Different parts of the Qt OpenAPI module are available under different
